{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "tr-06-overtaking-same-direction",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Overtaking Trains in the Same Direction",
    "description": "Subtract train speeds and use the sum of their lengths to model one train completely overtaking another.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start with sum-of-lengths over speed-difference, reverse for unknown speeds and lengths, then add same-start and head-start geometry, cancel common train length for speed ratios, and finish with two-walker inference.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 20, Problems on Trains. Approved scope: Teach overtaking as relative motion: the faster train must gain a distance equal to both train lengths, so use speed difference and sum of lengths. Cover time to overtake, unknown faster/slower speed, equal-length trains, trains starting together, an explicit head start, speed ratios obtained from same-direction versus opposite-direction crossing times, and the two-walker inference pattern as a transfer exercise."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Overtaking looks different from a head-on crossing, but only one sign changes.\n\nBoth train lengths still have to clear. Because both trains move the same way, the faster train gains only at the difference of their speeds.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Overtaking is the same crossing geometry with slower relative motion. The faster train must gain enough to move its entire length and the slower train's entire length past it.\n\nSo distance = L₁ + L₂, but relative speed = faster − slower.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example: 150 m and 100 m trains at 72 and 54 km/hr.\nRelative speed = 18 km/hr = 5 m/s.\nTotal length = 250 m.\nTime = 250/5 = 50 s.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
